   Without those invariants, from this method in isolation, we cannot *prove* that right.parent is never null.

Conclusion:
Based solely on the provided method and without the broader class invariants and initializations, it is not provable that right.parent (i.e., the expression "right" at the line "Node<K, V> center = right.parent;") is always non-null. It *can* be null under plausible initial conditions (e.g., empty stack before first add, specific size/leavesSkipped values) because there are no local null checks or structural guards.

